window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"","i":"ccs-101-communication-skills","x":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills University of Embu · Humanities This pack organizes the available course outline and lecture materials into a topic-by-topic study path. Search covers the text inside each note. Use the progress tracker to mark a topic complete only after you can explain it without looking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"Learning goals","i":"learning-goals","x":"Learning goals Work through each topic in order, or search for a specific term or example. Use the interactive recall checklist at the end of each note. Record confidence and revision dates in the progress tracker. Consult the source material when a note marks a gap or an equation needs checking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 01 · Fundamentals of Communication 02 · Communication and Collaboration 03 · Study Skills 04 · Writing Skills"},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — Communication Skills","h":"How to study","i":"how-to-study","x":"How to study Read one topic and mark its checklist as you complete each activity. Close the notes and write the key ideas from memory. Use search or “Find in note” to resolve specific gaps. Update your confidence and next review date in the progress tracker."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"","i":"your-study-plan","x":"Your study plan"},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Weekly study cycle","i":"weekly-study-cycle","x":"Weekly study cycle Preview the course outline and choose this week's topics. Study one or two notes per session. Do a closed-book recall after each note. Revisit weak topics within 48 hours. Complete a mixed-topic review at the end of the week."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Exam-week cycle","i":"exam-week-cycle","x":"Exam-week cycle Review the progress tracker and prioritize low-confidence topics. Rework examples without looking at their solutions. Search the notes for definitions and distinctions you cannot recall. Complete the question bank and record errors in the tracker. Protect time for sleep and a final light review."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 101 — topic map","h":"","i":"ccs-101-topic-map","x":"CCS 101 — topic map Use this page as a fast index, not a replacement for the notes. Search by definition, method, example, or phrase to jump into the relevant section. 01 · Fundamentals of Communication — notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.md 02 · Communication and Collaboration — notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.md 03 · Study Skills — notes/03_study-skills.md 04 · Writing Skills — notes/04_writing-skills.md"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"","i":"progress-tracker","x":"Progress tracker Mark a topic complete only when you can explain it without the notes. Use the local progress panel to see completed pages. Mark pages as done and they will appear here."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"Topic mastery","i":"topic-mastery","x":"Topic mastery"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"Topic mastery","i":"topic-mastery-2","x":"Topic mastery 01 · Fundamentals of Communication — Open note 02 · Communication and Collaboration — Open note 03 · Study Skills — Open note 04 · Writing Skills — Open note"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"Weak-topic action plan","i":"weak-topic-action-plan","x":"Weak-topic action plan Record the topic and what is unclear. Choose the next action and review date. Revisit the topic and raise your confidence score."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"","i":"01-fundamentals-of-communication","x":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication Source: CCS 101 Learning notes (Topic 1).txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes --- slide 2 --- 19/09/21 20 Topic 2/11: Listening and Note Taking 27 September, 2021 7:00 – 10:00 am (Virtual) --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Principles/Properties of communication Communication is contextual Communication takes place in a given space - p sychological context (personality, value), s ituational context (time and place), e nvironmental context (physical features – noise level Communication is complicated It involves making several choices. Communication is dynamic Always changing, or taking place 19/09/21 14 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration The place of collaboration in effective communication: Collaboration defined - Collaboration is the process where two or more people, entities or organizations work together to complete a task or achieve a goal. Considering that communication is aimed at achieving specific goals, collaboration is therefore central. Benefits of collaboration : Improves delivery of services Promotes team work Innovation Effectiveness Improved communication 20/09/21 19 Through listening to and learning from team members, you can help each other reach"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"romotes team work Innovation Effectiveness Improved communication 20/09/21 19 Through listening to and learning from team members, you can help each other reach your goals --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Barriers to communication Differences in opinion Jumping into conclusions Stereotyping – treating people as though they are the same Lack of knowledge Lack of interest Difficult with expression Emotions Personality 19/09/21 17 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration The 7Cs of effective communication Clarity – be clear and not vague Completeness Concise Consistency Correctness Consideration Courtesy 19/09/21 18 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Expected Lesson Outcomes: Learners who are able to articulate what constitutes effective communication and its relevance in life Learners who are able to d iscuss the elements that constitute communication Learners who are to e xplain the importance of communication in academics and in life in general Learners who are able to articulate the principles of communication Learners who are able to discuss the various types of"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"on in academics and in life in general Learners who are able to articulate the principles of communication Learners who are able to discuss the various types of communication 19/09/21 4 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Exercise 1: Your County Governor intends to recruit a communication expert to draft and disseminate development initiatives. You have been requested to Chair the recruitment panel. In preparation for the exercise, prepare a well structured interview points the panel should consider in selecting the most suitable candidate. 19/09/21 2 --- slide 2 --- 19/09/21 1 Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and C ollaboration 20 September, 2021 7:00 – 10:00 am (Virtual) --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Expected Lesson Outcomes: Learners who are able to articulate what constitutes effective communication and its relevance in life Learners who are able to d iscuss the elements that constitute communication Learners who are to e xplain the importance of communication in academics and in life in general Learners who are able to articulate the principles of communication Learners who are able"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"in the importance of communication in academics and in life in general Learners who are able to articulate the principles of communication Learners who are able to discuss the various types of communication 19/09/21 5 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Lesson Objectives: To enable the learners think through on what constitutes effective communication and why collaboration is important in academic and general life To enable the learners reflect on the principles of communication To create a deeper understanding on the part of the learners the various types of communication To enable the learners reflect on the barriers to effective communication 19/09/21 3 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration What is communication: Summary It is a process It involves more than one entity There is something to be exchanged or shared It is aimed at achieving specific goal(s) May take place using different approaches – verbal, non-verbal, symbols, signs or behavior; NOTE Even animals in the wild do communicate using different approaches 19/09/21 8 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"; NOTE Even animals in the wild do communicate using different approaches 19/09/21 8 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration What is communication: C ommunication is a process by which meaning i s exchanged between individuals or an individual and a group through a common system of s ymbo ls , signs an d behavio r. S ending , giving, or exchanging information and ideas , which is often expressed non-verbally and verbally Communication is also a process of sharing or exchanging ideas, information, knowledge, attitude or feelings through certain signs, symbols and behavior. Therefore; Communication is more than transfer of ideas and thoughts, it is a dynamic process of action and interaction towards desired goals . 19/09/21 7 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Why study communication? We are social beings and communication is central in shaping our relations and interactions It helps in understanding and gaining insight of oneself and others – intrapersonal & interpersonal communication It helps us build self esteem and self-confidence – through the various messages one receives from others about self."},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ntrapersonal & interpersonal communication It helps us build self esteem and self-confidence – through the various messages one receives from others about self. Studying communication and using it effectively can lead to more positive feelings of ourselves, helps in character management, image safe guarding 19/09/21 9 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Types of communication Communication can be categorized in two ways. By the number of people involved in the communication process/ Levels of communication By the channel or medium used in the communication process A: By the number of people involved: ( i ) Intrapersonal communication (ii) Inter-personal communication – dyadic and small group communication (iii) Public communication – an individual communicating to many people (iv) Mass communication 19/09/21 15 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Why study communication? Cont’d It increases our knowledge about human relationships because studying communication includes learning about how people relate with one another and about what communication is appropriate for a given situation. Enables us to"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"udying communication includes learning about how people relate with one another and about what communication is appropriate for a given situation. Enables us to acquire life and survival skills – public speaking, strategic decision-making Contributes to professional growth – ability to listen and analyze messages Poor communication may lead to misunderstanding, frustration, being ignored by others, unsuccessful careers, among, many other negative consequences 19/09/21 10 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Principles/Properties of communication Communication is inescapable Communication is always occurring in human life at various levels, for instance, at the intra-personal level when you are thinking, planning, meditating, observing, gathering information and making conclusions. It also occurs at the interpersonal level as you interact with one another Communication is irreversible Cannot take back something once it is communicated Communication is dynamic Always changing, or taking place 19/09/21 13 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Types of communication By the channel or medium used in the"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"taking place 19/09/21 13 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Types of communication By the channel or medium used in the communication process Verbal communication - oral-auditory signal for communication Graphic communication - visual communication e.g., graphs, cartoons, models Written communication Non-verbal communication – paralinguistic communication and would include facial expressions, postures, gestures 19/09/21 16 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Key elements of communication – Cont’d Feedback This is the receiver's verbal, non-verbal or written response to the message from the source Code This is the systematic arrangement of symbols used by the source or receiver to create meaning in the mind of another person(s) or receiver(s) – words, non-verbal, grammar, expressions Encoding and decoding Encoding is the act of putting ideas and thoughts into a code, while decoding is interpreting/making sense of those ideas or thoughts. 19/09/21 12 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Flow of the lesson: Definition of communication Why study communication Key"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"09/21 12 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Flow of the lesson: Definition of communication Why study communication Key elements of communication Principles of communication Relevance of communication in academic life + life in general Types of communication Communication pathways Barriers to effective communication The role collaboration in enhancing effective communication 19/09/21 6 --- slide 2 --- Topic 1/11: Fundamentals of Communication and Collaboration Key elements of communication People Participants in the process May be the sources of the messages (conceive, packages, encodes and disseminate) Receivers of the messages (decodes, comprehends, attaches meaning, Factors that influence how messages are communicated: knowledge, interest and emotional state Message This is the verbal or non-verbal form of idea, thought or feeling that one person (source) wishes to communicate to a group of people (receivers) – the content Channel This is the means by which a message moves from a source to a receiver. It is also called the medium of transmission. 19/09/21 11"},{"u":"notes/01_fundamentals-of-communication.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Fundamentals of Communication","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"","i":"02-communication-and-collaboration","x":"02 · Communication and Collaboration Source: Lesson 2.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes Topic 2/11: Listening and Note Taking14 September, 20267.00 10.00 am Expected learning outcomesBy the end of this lesson, you will be able to:1.Describe the meaning of listening and its importance2.Explain the levels of listening3.Discuss the characteristics of a good listener & how to improve the skill4.Identify barriers of effective listening in varied contexts and how to overcome them.5.Explain the meaning of taking notes6.Take notes in various situations7.Take notes that show relative importance of ideas using different formats8.Code information in diagrammatic forms such as mind maps, charts, tables, social grams among others. Lesson content/flow of the lesson1.Meaning of listening2.Levels of listening3.Qualities of a good listener4.Barriers of effective listening5.Note taking6.Guidelines to note taking7.Note formats Definition of ListeningListening is different from hearing.Hearingis about receiving sound. Listeninggoes beyond. It involves constructing meaning and responding to messages.Listening means paying attention and making a conscious effort to process what you hear.It is active, deliberate and selective process of receiving aural stimuli.We can therefore"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ying attention and making a conscious effort to process what you hear.It is active, deliberate and selective process of receiving aural stimuli.We can therefore say, listening is a voluntary process that goes beyond simply reacting to sounds and includes understanding, analyzing, evaluating and responding. Importance of ListeningEssential for effective communicationVital for academic and professional successBuilding and maintaining relationshipsLinked to memory retentionCrucial in determining honesty in communicationReduces misunderstandings Varying levels of concentration in listening depends on:The purpose of listening.Physical and psychological state.Your background knowledge.The content of the speech or lecture.Levels of Listening Types of ListeningActive ListeningReflective listeningCritical listeningEmpathic listeningListening for enjoymentPassive listening Qualities of a good listenerThey talk less and listen moreThey do not interrupt othersThey are aware of biases and try to control themPlans responses after others have finished speaking. NOT while they are speaking.Never daydream or become preoccupied with their own thoughts when others talk.Provide feedbackAnalyzes the"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"s have finished speaking. NOT while they are speaking.Never daydream or become preoccupied with their own thoughts when others talk.Provide feedbackAnalyzes the conversation by looking at all relevant factors and asking open ended questions. How to improve the Listening skillMaintain eye contactControl the listening environmentObserve non-verbal communicationListen without judgingShow that you understandBecome an active listenerListen without thinking of other things Reading for background informationRepeating names during introductionsAsking clarifying questionsTaking notesRecording lectures (When allowed)How to improve the Listening skill cont Barriers to listeningare any obstacles or challenges that prevent a person from accurately receiving, understanding and processing the message sent by a speaker.Can be grouped into: 1.Physical/ Environmental barriers loud noise, interruptions, hooting etc.2.Psychological barriers stress, anger, anxiety, lack of interest etc.3.Physiological barriers hearing impairment, tiredness, illness etc. 4.Semantic barriers occurs when people misunderstand words or the language used by the speaker. Characterized by ambiguous words, jargon etc. Barriers"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ness etc. 4.Semantic barriers occurs when people misunderstand words or the language used by the speaker. Characterized by ambiguous words, jargon etc. Barriers to effective Listening Methods to overcome barriersConcentrate and maintain focusManage internal and external noiseResist talking when you should be listeningKeep an open mind Note taking is the practice of recording key pieces of information from another source such as a book or a meeting Record pertinent information such as:DateTitle of the presentationName of presenter or facilitatorUse economical ways of taking notes such as:AbbreviationsSymbolsAcronyms (words formed from the first letters or syllables of other words)Omit some letters such as vowels from wordsLeave out articles (a, and, the)Guidelines to Note Taking You should give your notes a structure to make them more intelligible to you. Some of the common formats are Cornell, diagram (mind mapping, charting)and outline.Note Formats Involves dividing the page: cues, notes and summaries.Source: lifehacker.comCornell format This is visual representation of connected ideas.Diagram -Mind mapping format Organizing notes in columns for different aspects.Charting format"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"omCornell format This is visual representation of connected ideas.Diagram -Mind mapping format Organizing notes in columns for different aspects.Charting format Hierarchy of ideasOutline format Top 10 Tips on Note Taking During Lectures1.2.Pay attention3.Underline, highlight and capitalize4.Use Shorthand (Abbreviations)5.Put distractions away6.Be comfortable7.Ask questions when confused8.Share and compare notes with classmates9.Prepare before lecture10.Have a consisted structure ETIVITIES By clicking on this link https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CdROC4CThCwwatch the video and answer the following questions.1. What was the aim of 17th annual meeting and conference?2. Universities are important in any society. Highlight 3 of the significance he mentioned?3. Which recommendation did he make as far as institutions of higher learning are concerned?4. How many times did you listen to the above video in order to respond to the listed questions?5. Which level of listening best describes you?6. Using any note-format, prepare a summary to highlight the main points in his message?"},{"u":"notes/02_communication-and-collaboration.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communication and Collaboration","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"","i":"03-study-skills","x":"03 · Study Skills Source: Topic five STUDY SKILLS updated.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes --- slide 2 --- Short-Term Memory (STM): Holds limited information for a brief period (about 20-30 seconds). Immediate memory is part of STM, storing around 7 chunks of data Long-Term Memory (LTM): Has unlimited capacity and duration. Proper encoding and organization are essential for efficient retrieval of stored information. --- slide 2 --- Memory Techniques To perform well in courses, you need to use strategies that aid in acquisition , retention, and retrieval of the information: Three key stages of memory: Sensory Memory , Short-Term Memory (STM) , and Long-Term Memory (LTM) Sensory Memory: Registers stimuli from the environment for a very brief time (milliseconds to seconds). Attention is required to transfer information to short-term memory . --- slide 2 --- D iscussion groups Choose your members wisely who are committed and those with common interests. Manageable group, about 4-8 people and a group leader. The least the better. Members who are time conscious and allow each other a chance to discuss (active participation --- slide 2 --- Survey Question Read Recall/ recite review Survey : Skim the chapter headings, diagrams to get a general idea of the content"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ctive participation --- slide 2 --- Survey Question Read Recall/ recite review Survey : Skim the chapter headings, diagrams to get a general idea of the content and organization. Question : Formulate questions about the content or the topic your are reading. Read , : Actively read to find answers of question formulated Recite: tell yourself the answers and write key points. Review: go through the material, reread the notes aloud to reinforce understanding. --- slide 2 --- PQ4R Method Preview: Skim the material . Read the titles, headings & highlighted text. Question: Think through questions about the material. Read: As you read, find answers to your questions. Reflect: Consider whether you have any unanswered questions or new questions. Recite: Speak aloud about the things you just read. Review: Look over the material one more time. --- slide 2 --- Study schedule: A study schedule is a time-management plan that will help you achieve your learning goals. In this plan, you’ll schedule your study sessions. Use Visual reminders, such as schedules hung on your room door or wall , to help keep tasks in your mind . However, motivation is essential to follow through on these plans ."},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"inders, such as schedules hung on your room door or wall , to help keep tasks in your mind . However, motivation is essential to follow through on these plans . Review and adjust the plan as planning is not a one-time activity. R egularly ask : What have I completed ? What remains unfinished ? Has a new task appeared ? Has any deadline changed ? Does my plan need adjustment? --- slide 2 --- Use Pomodoro technique A time-management method that helps improve focus and productivity by breaking study sessions into 25-minute intervals, followed by 5 -minute short breaks. You manage your time and make tasks seem more achievable by breaking them down into manageable chunks. You complete revision tasks in a short time frame, whilst giving your brain a breather, you’ll be ticking things off your list without becoming overwhelmed by too much information . Setting yourself a time limit makes you more productive . --- slide 2 --- P lanning - deciding what needs to be done, how it will be done, when it will be done, and how much time should be allocated to it . H ow you spend your time daily is key to improving productivity and achieving goals. Prepare a Time Log - Identify daily life"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"h time should be allocated to it . H ow you spend your time daily is key to improving productivity and achieving goals. Prepare a Time Log - Identify daily life activities like sleep, eating, travel, errands, to assessing time allocation. Calculating time spent on these activities can reveal available time for other tasks. Time Log is a record of what you do each hour of the day for one week Make daily or weekly schedule --- slide 2 --- By identifying the real reason for your concentration problems, you’ll be able to select the appropriate strategy to overcome each of your concentration problems. For example, Develop a positive attitude, creating interest in the task, setting goals , and scheduling your assignments . Create a Positive Learning Environment to avoid External distractions --- slide 2 --- iv. set mini- deadlines A student should ask himself/herself: When is this task due ? What happens if I miss the deadline ? How much time will the task require ? However , be warned that deadline alone is not enough . You have to consider the workload Review completed tasks at the end of the day as it allows for reflection on productivity and ensure you update task priorities"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"You have to consider the workload Review completed tasks at the end of the day as it allows for reflection on productivity and ensure you update task priorities regularly. --- slide 2 --- Topic 5 STUDY SKILLS Date:28 th , September, 2026 By Peris Mwihaki , Patrick Miringo and Juliet Mukhwana --- slide 2 --- Importance of study skills Effective study skills improve academic performance, reduce stress, and foster lifelong learning habits. Academic Success : Study skills improve ability to learn, organization, retention, and application of knowledge, crucial for thriving in self-directed college learning. Life Skills : Study skills extend beyond academics, improving time management, organization, and problem-solving in various life areas . --- slide 2 --- Stress Reduction : Effective strategies reduce last- minute cramming and help students cope with academic stress. Help increase your confidence, self-esteem, and reduce anxiety about tests, exams and meeting assignment deadlines. NB: D eveloping solid study skills early on can make a big difference in students’ academic performance and personal growth. Study skills are crucial not only for managing coursework but also for balancing"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"an make a big difference in students’ academic performance and personal growth. Study skills are crucial not only for managing coursework but also for balancing other areas of life. --- slide 2 --- Expected Learning Outcomes Apply effective time-management and study techniques to improve academic performance . Engage actively with learning materials by asking questions, reflecting, and connecting ideas. Set academic priorities, develop study plans, and use planning tools to avoid last-minute cramming and procrastination. Identify personal learning preferences and adapt study strategies to individual strengths and needs . --- slide 2 --- Definition Studying is the process of acquiring knowledge or skills through reading, reflection, and practice to enhance understanding, retention, recall and application. Study skills are techniques, methods, habits and approaches that enhance learning. They help students receive, process, organize, comprehend, retain , recall and apply information efficiently in different circumstances . --- slide 2 --- Memory Techniques Use acronyms - a word that is made up by taking the first letters of all the key words or ideas you need to remember and"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ces . --- slide 2 --- Memory Techniques Use acronyms - a word that is made up by taking the first letters of all the key words or ideas you need to remember and creating a new word out of them. Acronym (ROYGBIV) -the colour spectrum Make up a Sentence in which the first letter of each word is part of or represents the initial of what you want to remember. For example mnemonics of Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain to remember the order of the colours of a rainbow Visual image – Associate a visual image with a word or name to help you remember them better. Chunking grouping different bits of information together into more manageable or meaningful chunks . --- slide 2 --- Studying by Reading NB: Simply reading and re-reading notes or textbooks feels productive but does little for long-term retention. It’s a passive activity. Hence you should actively engage with the material by use of strategies/methods like ; 1. SQ3R strategy to study SQ3R is a reading tool specially designed to help you absorb information from books and articles. It is designed to increase your retention of what you read by setting study goals and giving you techniques to help fix information in your mind a"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"and articles. It is designed to increase your retention of what you read by setting study goals and giving you techniques to help fix information in your mind a reading --- slide 2 --- Memory process Encoding: Interpreting information in a meaningful way. Without encoding, information cannot be stored effectively. Storage : Organizing and repeating information so it can be placed into long-term memory (LTM). Active engagement is necessary for effective storage . Retrieval : Accessing information from LTM. Effective retrieval depends on how well the information was stored. --- slide 2 --- Tips to consider before you start to Study Studying is a skill that can be mastered like many others. First, understand the process, then develop appropriate techniques . ii. (Self-awareness) Start with yourself: What kind of student are you ? How do you like to tackle things? Think about what has worked for you in the past – are you an early bird or a night owl? Or neither? --- slide 2 --- continuation ii. prioritizing tasks using the ABC method , R ank and arrange tasks in order of priority: A = High priority B = Medium priority C = Low priority and utilize peak times for studying. Categorizing"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"method , R ank and arrange tasks in order of priority: A = High priority B = Medium priority C = Low priority and utilize peak times for studying. Categorizing tasks into A, B, and C helps clarify priorities, leading to more efficient time management and productivity. Focus on A tasks first for high optimal results . B for medium and C for the Least priority tasks. Create a list of rewards for completing tasks to enhance motivation. --- slide 2 --- NOTE-TAKING The key is not to copy down chunks of text from a book or your notes. Your aim is to make clear notes using a few of your own words. Use an Effective Note-Taking method from lectures: Use structured methods like the outline method, Cornell system and mind mapping for better organization and retention . Reviewing your Notes before class starts; by reciting them out loud. --- slide 2 --- Compare: Show similarities between items. Compare verbal and non-verbal communication . Contrast: Highlight differences between items. Contrast interpersonal and mass communication. Differentiate: Point out clear differences. Differentiate between formal and informal communication. Evaluate: Judge value, weigh pros and cons/strengths and"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"n. Differentiate: Point out clear differences. Differentiate between formal and informal communication. Evaluate: Judge value, weigh pros and cons/strengths and weaknesses, give conclusion. Evaluate group discussions in learning. Analyze: Break into parts, show relationships, explain significance. Analyze barriers to effective communication. --- slide 2 --- Illustrate: Use concrete examples, diagrams, or cases to explain. Illustrate how culture affects communication. Justify : Give reasons and evidence to support an opinion. Justify the use of PowerPoint in teaching. Summarize: Present only the main points briefly. Summarize the studying strategies/techniques . Critically examine: Explore strengths and weaknesses, then give balanced judgment. Critically examine the effectiveness of non-verbal cues in communication. --- slide 2 --- Prioritising techniques/methods: The central idea is not simply deciding what to do, but deciding what should be done first, what can wait, and what may not deserve much attention at all . Eisenhower Matrix Tasks are assessed according to both importance and urgency. Important and urgent Must be handled immediately Important but not urgent Matters, but"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"hower Matrix Tasks are assessed according to both importance and urgency. Important and urgent Must be handled immediately Important but not urgent Matters, but there is still time Urgent but less important Demands attention but contributes less to major goals Neither urgent nor important Adds little value --- slide 2 --- TIME MANAGEMENT Time management What is time management? What does effective time management involve? Time management is the ability to plan, organize, and control how you spend your time to achieve your goals . prioritising , planning , organising , focusing , and balancing activities without becoming overwhelmed. --- slide 2 --- TIME MANAGEMENT benefits of effective time management Academic: Better grades by planning your study routines and completing assignments on time Improved focus during study sessions Reduces last-minute cramming with more time to revise and prep Mental : Lower stress and anxiety from workload Better sleep and energy level Increased confidence and self-discipline Prevents burnout Career preparation: Learning to meet deadlines, prioritize tasks, and manage responsibilities prepares students for the demands of the workplace. --- slide 2 ---"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"areer preparation: Learning to meet deadlines, prioritize tasks, and manage responsibilities prepares students for the demands of the workplace. --- slide 2 --- Important and Urgent DO NOW Important but not Urgent SCHEDULE Urgent but less important DELEGATE Neither Urgent nor Important LIMIT --- slide 2 --- iii. B reaking tasks into manageable parts You may identify an important task but still fails to act because the task seems too broad hence you are supposed to break it into various parts. For example instead of; a broad task like, “Write Communication Skills term paper,” You can break it into: Understand the question. Identify key terms . Search for sources . Read three sources . Prepare an outline . Draft the introduction . Write the main sections and edit. Reference and submit --- slide 2 --- STUDY SMARTER NOT JUST HARDER THANK YOU --- slide 2 --- Overall here are 10 Ways to Make an Effective Study Plan/schedule Know your learning style – Every student has a different learning style hence know when, where, and how you study best, and tailor your plan around that. Check your commitments – Balance study with work, classes, and activities. Allocate time per subject – Plan hours"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"study best, and tailor your plan around that. Check your commitments – Balance study with work, classes, and activities. Allocate time per subject – Plan hours for each class; set early deadlines. Create a schedule – Add study sessions to your calendar like appointments. Set realistic goals – Break big goals into smaller, achievable tasks. Make it routine – Turn study into a daily habit for consistency. Include breaks – Rest, move, or refresh to boost focus. Try Pomodoro / ABC method to focus on a given task. Study with others – Use or groups for accountability. Review and adjust –Regularly update your plan to fit your needs. --- slide 2 --- E- tivity Write four tasks for this week. Mark them as Do, Schedule, Delegate or Limit Evaluate the role of a study schedule in improving academic performance . Define active studying and explain why it is more effective than passive studying . Discuss the importance of time management in examination preparation, giving practical examples . Examine your best study methods, time and place . --- slide 2 --- Common Exam Terms and Their Meanings Define: Give the exact meaning, short and precise. Define communication. Describe: Give details about"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ace . --- slide 2 --- Common Exam Terms and Their Meanings Define: Give the exact meaning, short and precise. Define communication. Describe: Give details about features, steps, or characteristics. Describe the process of effective listening. Explain: Make something clear by showing how/why it happens. Explain why non-verbal communication is important. Discuss: Present arguments for and against, examine, then conclude by giving your opinion. Discuss the impact of social media on communication. --- slide 2 --- Active learning therefore involves; Retrieval practice or self-testing Explaining concepts in one's own words Creating concept maps Connecting new ideas with prior knowledge Generating examples Teaching or discussing material with another student Study ahead of the lecturer. All these help improve and promote understanding, long-term retention, and application of knowledge. --- slide 2 --- Organize your notes by module or topic. Determine the subjects you need to focus on. Utilize past exam papers to steer your study efforts. Consider how many questions you need to be answered for each paper, and practice writing responses to these questions . Maintain a positive attitude"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"dy efforts. Consider how many questions you need to be answered for each paper, and practice writing responses to these questions . Maintain a positive attitude towards your learning, Don't hesitate to seek assistance or clarification from your instructors when needed . --- slide 2 --- IMPROVING CONCENTRATION Concentration is divided concentration into three levels: light, moderate, and deep. There are three types of concentration problems: difficulty focusing at will, difficulty sustaining focus over a period of time, and difficulty limiting focus to one task at a time . The real causes of most concentration problems are lack of attention, lack of interest, and lack of motivation --- slide 2 --- Preparing for Exams Poor performance/failure in exams can often stem from anxiety or the overwhelming pressure of stress. Nevertheless, inadequate preparation and lack of planning are typically the main reasons behind exam failures. Therefore, it's crucial to take charge of your study schedule and implement strategies that can enhance your exam performance. Getting Started: Start early Create a practical revision timetable that includes breaks for social activities, allowing you to"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"an enhance your exam performance. Getting Started: Start early Create a practical revision timetable that includes breaks for social activities, allowing you to unwind. --- slide 2 --- S- specific: be clear and precise on what you want to achieve, not vague or general goals. For example, M- measurable: you can determine you have made progress and succeeded A- achievable: even if the goal is challenging you can do it with efforts, motivation and resources available R- realistic/relevant: you must align the goal set with your career path, abilities, and long-term plans T- Time is limited or bound: have a deadline or timeframe --- slide 2 --- Continuation for tips iii . Create an effective study area- make sure your study area is free from distractions/interruptions, well lit, ventilated and that you have the necessary learning materials Attend lectures regularly and sit in the front or near the front of the class to avoid distraction. Eat properly , maintain a balanced diet, and take regular exercise Set SMART goals. --- slide 2 --- Boost productivity with SMART goals that help focus effort and achieve better results over time. Gain clarity and motivation: SMART goals bring"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"--- slide 2 --- Boost productivity with SMART goals that help focus effort and achieve better results over time. Gain clarity and motivation: SMART goals bring structure to ambition, making it easier to track progress, stay motivated, and build confidence that milestones can be reached. Tailor goals to fit your life: SMART goals ensure personal and professional ambitions align with your current skills, timeline and resources. Avoid goal-setting pitfalls by regularly evaluating and adapting your goals to keep them effective and flexible . --- slide 2 --- ACTIVE LEARNING Distinguish between active and passive learning. passive learning, The student listens to lectures, takes notes, memorizes and reproduces the material for the required assessments without really reflecting upon it . active learning The student becomes much more involved in the material being presented, critically evaluating it, thinking through the implications, noting questions and issues, and trying to relate it to other observations or information of their own . --- slide 2 --- STUDY SKILLS Some of the skills that we will discuss include; Active Learning Time management Note-taking Improving concentration Memory"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"r own . --- slide 2 --- STUDY SKILLS Some of the skills that we will discuss include; Active Learning Time management Note-taking Improving concentration Memory techniques Studying by Reading Group discussion"},{"u":"notes/03_study-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Study Skills","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"","i":"04-writing-skills","x":"04 · Writing Skills Source: Topic 8 Writing Skills.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes --- slide 36 --- Checklist for Arguments Do I have a strong and clear statement in the introduction (thesis statement) that announces to the reader my proposition (the view I intend to put forth for debate)? Are there words that I need to define before getting into the argument? Have I paraded, in a logical manner, sufficient evidence to support my point of view? (facts, statistics, definitions, examples, anecdotes and so on). Have I handled objections wisely by acknowledging the opposing side and attempting to show why it is weaker than the side I am proposing? Have I acknowledged sources of information for any ideas that are not from my own head? Have I used words appropriately; that is the right words for the intended meaning? Have I avoided distorting information by presenting it accurately and completely? Have I used the right tone of voice? For example, using a light warm tone for a light-hearted subject and serious tone for subjects such as war or ethnicity Have l avoided over-simplifying a complex issue by suggesting rather simple answers to resolve the issue. --- slide 36 --- Arguments have a conventional way in which they should be presented. This can be"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ssue by suggesting rather simple answers to resolve the issue. --- slide 36 --- Arguments have a conventional way in which they should be presented. This can be simplified as Claim (proposition) - Evidence - Argument and Counterargument. This involves: Making a claim or proposition (the statement of opinion that is presented for debate) . In the essay introduction, it is called a thesis statement , while in a paragraph it is a topic sentence. Example. most teenagers joining university today do so for reasons completely different from those for which universities have been established. Using supporting reasons and evidence (facts, cases, statistics, examples, authorities, anecdotes, scenarios, etc.). Handling counterarguments (involves acknowledging, accommodating and meeting opposing points of view). --- slide 36 --- B. The Body Paragraphs The body of an essay is made up of several paragraphs. Each paragraph attempts to advance or develop in more details the main idea that is stated or implied in the introduction. Each paragraph does this by methods that are determined by the overall purpose for writing that essay. There are various patterns used by writers to develop the idea in"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"agraph does this by methods that are determined by the overall purpose for writing that essay. There are various patterns used by writers to develop the idea in a given paragraph. These include definition, description, classification, argument, and cause and effect exemplification --- slide 36 --- Examples of thesis statements It is very exciting for anyone joining campus after high school, however, Surviving campus in 21 st century can be challenging and therefore requires knowledge and skills to navigate this space . Activity Draw a thesis statement for any essay topic --- slide 36 --- 8.7 What is an Argument? As a writing strategy, an argument involves the presentation of reasoning, evidence and logic in order to convince a reader to accept a particular point of view on a debatable topic. Formal argumentation relies more on authoritative opinion and logic to support a point of view and it appeals to the faculties of reasoning rather than emotions to convince the reader about an idea. --- slide 36 --- 2.Supporting Sentences These sentences present details, facts and examples that expand on or illustrate the main idea. Using Facts and Opinions A fact has to do with anything you"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"nces These sentences present details, facts and examples that expand on or illustrate the main idea. Using Facts and Opinions A fact has to do with anything you say that is an actuality, a certainty or truth. It represents an act, deed, or event that can be easily verified especially physically. An opinion on the other hand has to do more with a feeling, a view, or a belief. 1. Warthogs are the ugliest animals on earth. (Fact/Opinion) 2. All cultures have greeting rituals. (Fact/Opinion) 3. That law was amended in 2015. (Fact/Opinion) 4. Football is more interesting to watch than rugby. (Fact/Opinion) 5. 2016 is leap year. (Fact/Opinion) Concluding Sentence These sums up the paragraph's main idea or what has been said. Alternatively, it draws a logical conclusion from the main idea. --- slide 36 --- What is a paragraph? Cont … Topic/thesis Sentence: This is the first sentence and it expresses the main idea that unifies the paragraph. A good topic sentence does two things: It tells the reader what you are going to discuss in the paragraph (the topic) It establishes control so that you do not wander away from the basic purpose of your paragraph (controlling idea). Examples Colour"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"in the paragraph (the topic) It establishes control so that you do not wander away from the basic purpose of your paragraph (controlling idea). Examples Colour filters offer three advantages in black-and- white photography. Overeating, which is becoming an attractive pastime for the affluent of the world, has several dangerous effects. --- slide 36 --- A. Introduction It is usually written mainly in order to announce to your reader your main intention or purpose for writing the essay. As academic essays are written in order to inform or to persuade an audience, therefore, your introduction should aim at making you are the reader to be interested in reading the essay. The most important sentence in the introduction is the thesis statement or your statement of intention. It is this sentence more than any other that determines what you will say in the rest of the essay. It is usually referred to as the controlling sentence of the entire essay. It is usually the last sentence in the introduction and it establishes a smooth link between the introduction and the body. --- slide 36 --- Features of a Good Paragraph A paragraph is a group of sentences developing a main idea. A"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"a smooth link between the introduction and the body. --- slide 36 --- Features of a Good Paragraph A paragraph is a group of sentences developing a main idea. A well-written paragraph in an informative or argumentative essay should be: Unified: All sentences clearly relate to the main idea of the paragraph. Coherent: All sentences should be logically connected to each other. Fully developed: Paragraphs should have sufficient details to explain or expand the point or claim made in the paragraph. Use examples, facts, opinions, statistics, quotations, definitions, and so on. Appropriately organized: Paragraphs should have a clear logical pattern of organization such as chronological, spatial, emphatic, narrative, cause-effect and comparison-contrast. Writers do not always use topic sentences because they have the skill to carry the reader along without explicit signals. --- slide 36 --- 8.8 Tackling Exam Essay Questions Preview the exam and choose the questions to answer. Plan your time according to the worth of the questions Start with the easiest question because you get a psychological boost if you are able to successfully accomplish a task and therefore you would be motivated to"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Start with the easiest question because you get a psychological boost if you are able to successfully accomplish a task and therefore you would be motivated to tackle more difficult questions. When stalled for an answer, move on to another question or take a quick break. Keep your exam time until the last possible moment. --- slide 36 --- Expository essays are those that set out to explain something. a.Analytic expositions: They entail breaking material into constituent parts in order to show the hidden nature and how the parts relate to each other. comparison-contrast expositions: these show the similarities and differences of an idea, object or thing. cause-effect expositions : Many things can be explained .by identifying what causes them or alternatively explaining the effect, for instance diseases. 5 Argumentative essays involves convincing others of the correctness of a given view. --- slide 36 --- ETIVITIES Consider the following essay topics : 1. Competence Based Curriculum (CBC);pros, cons and way forward. 2.Surviving campus in the 21 st century. 3.Dealing with a pandemic requires a lifestyle change. 4.The youth can help end political violence in Kenya. 5.Personality"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"urviving campus in the 21 st century. 3.Dealing with a pandemic requires a lifestyle change. 4.The youth can help end political violence in Kenya. 5.Personality traits majorly dictates career choices. 6.Social Media ;the good and the ugly Pick any essay topic from the list above and tackle the tasks below: 1. Derive a thesis statement for the essay of choice 2. Write out four topic sentences for the four body paragraphs 3.Prepare an outline for the above essay. 4.Write a six paragraph essay --- slide 36 --- Definition of Common Verbs in Essay Questions Account for: to explain, as a cause and its effect; to justify Analyze: to examine the parts of and determine their .connections Clarify: to explain; to present details, reasons or examples Defend, justify or support:: to give reasons for; to offer evidence Define: to give the meaning; to describe basic characteristics Discuss: to examine and consider; to present details and reasons Evaluate: to examine advantages and disadvantages; rate or judge Identify: to define; to give the characteristics of Trace: to track or explain in chronological order --- slide 36 --- 8.6 Types of Essays 1.Descriptive Essays. Effective descriptive essays"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"the characteristics of Trace: to track or explain in chronological order --- slide 36 --- 8.6 Types of Essays 1.Descriptive Essays. Effective descriptive essays create word pictures of objects, persons, scenes, events or situations, in creating images it is important to create sensory impression 2. Narrative Essays Narration is a process of relating events that occurred where they occurred, when they occurred and who was involved and probably why. 3. 3Persuasive Essays Persuasive essays are intended to encourage others to adopt a view or take a particular action --- slide 36 --- Transitional Paragraphs If you are writing a lengthy essay, you may need a short paragraph or sentence that indicates a shift from one group of ideas to another. Transitional paragraphs play a number of functions such as to summarize the ideas that have so far been discussed, repeat the key ideas of the essay and indicate how new ideas may follow with the information presented in the preceding paragraph(s). --- slide 36 --- Example of topic sentences Firstly, surviving campus in the 21 st century requires that you have financial management skills. ACTIVITY In the public chat write topic sentences for your"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"es Firstly, surviving campus in the 21 st century requires that you have financial management skills. ACTIVITY In the public chat write topic sentences for your essay. --- slide 36 --- D.The Concluding Paragraph The purpose of a conclusion is to bring the essay “home to rest” by signaling that the discussion has been completed. The academic essay, does benefit from a conclusion that echoes the main point. To be effective, a conclusion must logically and stylistically connect with what the paper is all about. A long complex essay may end with a summary of key points, but shorter papers can end using some of the methods such as restating the thesis statement. That means repeating the purpose that was at first mentioned in the introduction in different wording, for example-question, a prediction or a warning. Example of a conclusion: As seen from the above discussion, practicing good listening skills is difficult. The important lesson, however, is that if we are going to initiate and maintain good human relationships with others, we have no choice but to strive and improve our listening habits. --- slide 36 --- Transitional Paragraphs cont. Although the three factors we have examined"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"rs, we have no choice but to strive and improve our listening habits. --- slide 36 --- Transitional Paragraphs cont. Although the three factors we have examined above- illiteracy, disease and malnutrition, help - explain the magnitude of the problems facing children in difficult circumstances there is a fourth problem that seems to override all the rest. War now poses the greatest challenges of all. Note: A transition paragraph stands on its own, separating as it were, two different sections of the essay, while a transition sentence is usually part of the paragraph that is concluding or introducing a new section --- slide 36 --- Features of Academic Writing cont. 5. It is a lengthy as well as creative process involving various stages such as choosing a topic, focusing the topic, gathering ideas, determining a structure , drawing an outline, drafting, revising, editing and proof-reading. It uses special jargon or vocabulary instead of general vocabulary thus you have to be keen on the register. 7. It acknowledges the ideas of others thus you are expected to use your ideas but also books and journals. You acknowledge other by in text citations and reference lists. --- slide 36 ---"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"of others thus you are expected to use your ideas but also books and journals. You acknowledge other by in text citations and reference lists. --- slide 36 --- Features of Academic Writing cont … 2. It avoids contractions such as “can’t”, “don’t”, “won’t”, “shouldn’t”, etc. 3. It avoids clichés, slang, colloquialism. These are expressions such as “The poor folks” “the kids”, etc. “kudos to our dear country”, “too many idlers”. 4. It is aimed at other academics. The academic audience is usually regarded as skeptical: one that is hard to convince or one that does not accept other people's ideas without seriously considering their intellectual value. It means then as a writer for this audience, you have to take extra caution in order to present information in a manner that will win approval from your audience. --- slide 36 --- Stages of Writing cont … organizing ideas and determine a structure. There are various ways to organize ideas : a) Chronological order - it is sometimes called historical order . It presents the material in the order in which it occurred or occurs in time; Spatial (or place) order-facts are presented on a geographical basis - from place to place, from north to"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"l in the order in which it occurred or occurs in time; Spatial (or place) order-facts are presented on a geographical basis - from place to place, from north to south, top to bottom, left to right, high to low, in and out, up and down or near to far. This method is effective for describing machinery, buildings, furniture, or geographical location. Order of importance - this presents material either in descending order of importance (deductive order): starting with the most important point to gain the reader's attention; or in ascending order (inductive order): starting with the least important point. --- slide 36 --- 8.3.3 The Stages of the academic writing Process /Planning an Essay Writing can be described as a process of discovering ideas, developing and organizing them. The process is not simple, and neither does it take place in a linear manner. It is, therefore, dynamic and recursive. No two people write in the same way. Some outline ideas before writing about them, while others do not. Some write their introduction first, while others do it at the end. Some revise their essay as they go along; some do it at a later point. As a student writer, you must discover the approach"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"first, while others do it at the end. Some revise their essay as they go along; some do it at a later point. As a student writer, you must discover the approach that works best for you on any particular occasion. --- slide 36 --- 8.3.2 Features of Academic Writing Academic writing is peculiar and is identified by certain characteristics: 1. Writers usually avoid the use of personal pronouns, I, you, he, me, she, him, and so rely more on the passive as opposed to the active voice. Examples Passive voice Active Voice The examination results are amazing. I am amazed by the examination results. The operation on the young girl was completed in two hours. I took two hours to operate on the young girl. In this paper ,the researcher sets out … I will look at features of academic writing --- slide 36 --- 8.2 Lesson Outcomes By the end of this lesson , you will be able to: 1.Explain the main features of academic writing. 2.Describe the stages of academic writing 3.Prepare an essay outline 4.Describe types of essay topics 5.Describe parts of an essay 6. Describe the features of a good paragraph 7.Write an essay in a given genre 8. Subject verb Agreement in sentences. --- slide 36 --- CCS"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"be parts of an essay 6. Describe the features of a good paragraph 7.Write an essay in a given genre 8. Subject verb Agreement in sentences. --- slide 36 --- CCS 101:COMMUNICATION SKILLS TOPIC 8 :ACADEMIC WRITING 1 & 2 Facilitators : Dr. Rose Burundi, Mr. Patrick Miringo and Ms. Everlyne Riimi Njagi Date 26 th October 2026 --- slide 36 --- 8.3.1 Uses of Academic Writing Writing is a way of thinking about a subject. The following are some of the reasons Budd (2005, p.3) gives for writing at this level: They make you think about an issue in depth; They help you develop the ability to think logically, to argue a case; They make you relate theoretical knowledge to particular issues; They help you to write fluently; They help you to write according to the demands of a particular discipline…. --- slide 36 --- 8.3 Academic Writing . Academic writing is a formal style of writing used in universities, research organization and scholarly publications. It is serious writing, guided by certain conventions such that you do not have the freedom to present your ideas in any way you like or are comfortable with. --- slide 36 --- Difference between planning and a plan Planning is a very important"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"freedom to present your ideas in any way you like or are comfortable with. --- slide 36 --- Difference between planning and a plan Planning is a very important stage, It is a mental activity and must be differentiated from a ‘plan’ or ‘outline’, which is a physical or written product of the mental activity. As a process, planning begins when you start analyzing your essay topic. It continues as you gather information and make notes, as you formulate a tentative structure in which you will organize your ideas and does not end until you complete the essay. It does not progress linearly but is a rather recursive process – meaning that as a writer you are not a slave to your plan. You produce a plan to use as a tool for writing and you can change or alter sections of it as you continue to discover more information about your subject. There is not one way of planning. At the center of each planning process, however, is the need to look for a suitable structure to order information. Whereas some essay topics have an inherent structure, others will require that you seriously consider the best mode of presenting your ideas . In writing an essay that describes a process, you cannot, for"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"tructure, others will require that you seriously consider the best mode of presenting your ideas . In writing an essay that describes a process, you cannot, for example impose your own order. --- slide 36 --- 8.4.3 Parts of an Essay Topic In order to decide accurately the kind of ideas, information, explanations, or facts to include in your essay, you need to identify the following from your topic be it a closed or open-ended one: Directive/instructional verbs (E.g. Describe, analyze, discuss, compare and contrast, etc.) Content words/Subject (E.g. Tourism, culture, environment, etc.). Aspect (Usually signaled by a phrase ending in ‘of’ e.g. economic effects of) Scope/limit/extent ((E.g. “three”, “four”, “never”, “always”, “in 1000 words”, “facing”, “before”, “ during”, “between 1800 and 1860”, “greatest impact”, etc. --- slide 36 --- 8.5.1What is a paragraph? Paragraphs are important building blocks in the writing of essays. A paragraph usually contains one main idea captured in a sentence, with about four to eight supporting sentences which expand the main idea by giving explanation, details and/or examples to support the main idea of the paragraph. A paragraph can be part of a"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ting sentences which expand the main idea by giving explanation, details and/or examples to support the main idea of the paragraph. A paragraph can be part of a longer piece of writing, as it is in an essay, or it can stand alone as in an exam answer. A paragraph is unified by a single main idea. There is no one correct structure of writing a paragraph, however, it should have three main components, namely: --- slide 36 --- 8.5 WRITING 2 : Sections of the Essay and Developing Effective Paragraphs An academic essay has three main subdivisions: 1 The introduction 2 The body 3.The conclusion. Each one of these contains main ideas and supporting details, all developed using paragraphs. --- slide 36 --- 8.4.2 Closed Topics Closed topics are more specific and often set a limit as to what you should say or do Look at the following examples: 1. Identify three major environmental problems facing Kenya today. Explain their nature. Say which is the most serious and why. 2. Using any relevant model of communication identify and explain five essential elements of the communication process. 3. Discuss the role of women in environmental conservation in a country of your choice. ­ --- slide 36"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ain five essential elements of the communication process. 3. Discuss the role of women in environmental conservation in a country of your choice. ­ --- slide 36 --- Stages of writing cont . Draw an outline. To arrive at a useful plan, consider the following planning strategies: Analyze your essay topic. Go over all the information you have collected i.e. your notes. Identify all key points. Think about an approximate sequence for your material. Draw up a tentative plan or outline. Outlining can be done manually or using computer software such as Omni outliner, Inspiration Example A five-paragraph essay comparing the pros and cons of social media. --- slide 36 --- Ascending order of complexity - simpler ideas first, followed by increasingly difficult or complex material. Descending order of familiarity - moving 'from the known to the unknown'. Cause and effect-put simply it means, \"because of this, then that\". That is, \"because this happened, that happened\". Topical - this requires that you deal with the material on a topic-by-topic basis. This is especially useful when there appears to be no real link between the chunks of material you have. --- slide 36 --- 8.4 Understanding"},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"opic-by-topic basis. This is especially useful when there appears to be no real link between the chunks of material you have. --- slide 36 --- 8.4 Understanding Essay Topics There is need to be aware that essay topics are usually presented in two forms: open-ended or closed essay topics. 8.4.1 Open-Ended Topics Such topics give a lot of freedom to you as a writer. How you respond to the topic is usually a matter of your own creativity and imagination. Consider the following. 1. “People are what they eat.” Discuss. 2. It is better to live a single life than to marry. 3. “A human being’s chief business on earth is to communicate.” Discuss. 4. “You don’t need a college degree today to be successful in life.” Discuss --- slide 36 --- Stages of Writing cont. Drafting : getting ideas and supporting details down on paper in rough form. Revising : rethinking and rewriting drafts to improve content, focus and structure and the general flow of ideas. Editing and proofreading: checking for effective word choice and sentence structure, correct grammar, spelling, punctuation and mechanics."},{"u":"notes/04_writing-skills.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Writing Skills","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"Question bank","h":"","i":"question-bank","x":"Question bank Use these prompts after studying a topic. Write your answer before searching the notes; then record what you missed in the progress tracker. Define the key terms introduced in each topic. Explain the central idea of each topic in your own words. Compare two related terms, processes, or methods from the course. Reproduce one worked example without referring to the source. Apply one idea from the course to a new example or context. Identify one misconception or error that could lose marks. Revisit every question you could not answer confidently."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"Answer key and self-assessment","h":"","i":"answer-key-and-self-assessment","x":"Answer key and self-assessment This pack is based on the provided course outline and lecture material. For open-ended questions, check the relevant topic note and the original source material. A complete answer should use accurate terminology, show the reasoning or method, and address every part of the prompt. Ready: correct explanation without notes. Review: mostly correct, but needed a prompt or missed an important step. Retry: could not explain the idea yet; revisit the note and try again. Keep corrections in the weak-topic action plan rather than copying answers without understanding them."}];